1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 So just change things up a little bit, just invite to the stage, Jim from Medtech.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
 Hopefully you'll be able to.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 Thank you very much, Jim, for standing in today.

4
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 It's our second standing of the day because of illness, that's our year.

5
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 So Jim, over to you.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 My microphone's on.

7
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 As you said, second standing of the day, standing in for Sam and also standing in for the previous presenter.

8
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 So I'm doing right.

9
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 Medtech, I'm the head of technology.

10
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 We look after public sector development processes.

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 We do the full end-to-end from policy design to managed service and everything in between.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 But as I said, I'm the head of tech.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 The engineers, cloud engineers, architects, et cetera.

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 So the full set.

15
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 I'm here to talk about what we've been doing with agents and how we're helping our teams to become more AI first, I guess.

16
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 We've been playing with ideas and concepts.

17
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 We've been trying to make it easier for people to pick up AI so that they don't have to do the hard work and learn how to do context engineering and prompt engineering and all the things that make this hard to do.

18
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 We've been doing that by building skills and agents for each job family.

19
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 So as you can see here, we've got a huge catalogue of skills that we've verified and built and tested with ideas and dummy projects.

20
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 We're starting to roll them out into customer projects now and starting to see a real impact of those.

21
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 We've been working with all of the different job families, focusing on the areas that cost the most, some definition of cost, and that's starting to see real improvements in speed.

22
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:47,000
 Of the 16 job families that we've been working with as an engineer, I find it hard to say that actually we're the least interesting part of that process.

23
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 We're kind of predictable.

24
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 Actually, a lot of the other job families are now becoming the constraint in that system.

25
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 I've realized over the last year or two that actually engineering has only ever been the constraint because we're quite expensive to scale as people.

26
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:15,000
 As code is becoming quick to produce, I'm not going to say free to produce, but as it's becoming quick and free to produce, we're moving that constraint elsewhere.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:28,000
 And so as code is not a bottleneck anymore, the constraints now shift into the idea generation and the idea testing and the development, deployment and testing environment.

28
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
 So engineering is where all of the hype and the investment has gone.

29
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
 We've been seeing tools. We're all using those tools every day, at least I hope you're starting to.

30
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 And that agentic workflow that we're all getting used to as engineers is really changing what we do and how we do it.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:55,000
 But everyone else in that system is now becoming that bottleneck and it's pushing that process out and across that system.

32
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:03,000
 And we're seeing that actually slowing things down and we're getting frustration built into that system.

33
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
 So an example of this, this is a real case.

34
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 A member of my team was asked to join sessions to try and analyze what was happening.

35
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 It was a delivery team on a real project.

36
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 They had a round of user research and that user research was, I think it was about a 50 page document of transcripts and insights and hypotheses and all of the good stuff that comes out of user research.

37
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:43,000
 The developer team estimated it was going to take about two weeks for them to produce the clickable prototypes to start taking that to the next round of user testing and going to test each of those hypotheses.

38
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:57,000
 Within a couple of hours, Lee had generated 10 clickable prototypes using the prototyping design skill that we put together and delivered it to the team and they took it through to the next round of testing.

39
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 It was a real demonstration to that team that they were a long way off being up to speed with where the developers were on that project.

40
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:17,000
 And as a set of product and user researchers and designers, they had that light bulb moment of actually we probably do need to start engaging with this process.

41
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 And that's why Lee was there on that from my team to start pushing that way of working and start talking.

42
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:39,000
 One of the issues that we've seen as part of this is how close engineers are to using these tools when they're doing their day job.

43
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:49,000
 You pick up your ticket, you check out the repo that you're working on, you go to VS Code and the agent's there in the tool that you've worked with for years and years and years.

44
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
 It's there for the present, all you do is just start working.

45
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
 And when I say agent, I don't mean auto quickly with LLMs, I mean actual agent workflows.

46
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:06,000
 Generally, we've been testing with Claude, but we've also made sure it works with Kira and Copilot and all of the others as well.

47
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 But yeah, it's there, it's front and present.

48
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
 When you open up your development environment, it's there already for you to use.

49
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
 And one of the problems that we've seen for other job families is that it isn't there.

50
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:29,000
 They're used to opening up a Word doc or going to a Teams meeting and getting a transcript from it or a recording of an interview that they've done or a Miro board.

51
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 And while there are some AI components in there, actually they're generic AI components and they don't really help them to do their job because they don't know what the job is that they do.

52
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:55,000
 And so there's a real disadvantage to not being part of that agentic workflow that because we as engineers have had that investment, we don't realise that they don't get that playground to look around with.

53
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:02,000
 So what we've been working with is creating a natural language way of interacting with our ways of working.

54
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:09,000
 We're building that into the tools that people use and we're starting to measure that process.

55
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:20,000
 So you can see here I've got Claude co-work there and that is enabling the designers to start using our agent.

56
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:27,000
 The agent is aware of all of our ways of working and all of the skills and you can describe what you're trying to do in natural language.

57
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:32,000
 And you don't have to learn that prompt engineering, you don't have to understand what a skill is.

58
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
 Just in the background it will go off, find that and start plugging into that process.

59
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
 All of those skills are verified, they've all been tested by the team that are developing them.

60
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:49,000
 There's a governance layer, there's an instrumented sort of set of recapturing with telemetry and the metrics around it as well.

61
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,000
 All of that's being stored so that we can see who's using what skills and when.

62
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:59,000
 We can record what the skills are doing or what they're looking at but we record that they've been used.

63
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:02,000
 And that's important so that we can see that uptake.

64
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:07,000
 We're starting to introduce it into those tools that they use.

65
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:12,000
 We're creating MCPs to cool our system so that things like Miro with the agents that are in there

66
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:17,000
 could start taking advantage of the skills that we're using as well.

67
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,000
 So starting to integrate it into those, that's kind of what we're working on now.

68
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,000
 And then as it says, how we're instrumenting.

69
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
 Generic agents are great, they're hugely useful.

70
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
 Much of this presentation was written using a generic agent.

71
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
 I had some skills to help me as well.

72
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:46,000
 And actually the other side of it is everyone probably has a playbook or a team charter or something that defines how you as a team work.

73
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:56,000
 But very few people read those every day and go back to them and study them and try and build them into the way that they work.

74
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:04,000
 Which, I don't know for you, but that's caused us some issues with inconsistency and sort of deviation and drift.

75
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
 But actually this is a huge advantage to us.

76
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:15,000
 We can now take those generic agents, give them specific knowledge of our ways of working through skills and through specific agents.

77
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:23,000
 And it means that those agents are amplifying what we do but they're also doing it in line with our ways of working.

78
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:32,000
 It doesn't mean the ways of working is correct, but at least we are using it and testing it and becoming more reproducible and more recurrent.

79
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:37,000
 And so that we can study change and get better at what we do each time.

80
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:43,000
 Governance, as you've just heard, is fundamental to this process.

81
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:58,000
 One of the things that we've agreed up front is that we need to store metadata about the agent, the skill, where it's been tested on, how it's been tested, what are the bounds of what it can do.

82
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
 An agent will very confidently assert that it can help you with a job.

83
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:08,000
 But if you haven't tested it and you don't know what it's good at doing with that skill, you have no idea what it's actually going to do.

84
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:17,000
 So we need to make sure that there's a human in the loop at the right point taking part of that action and delivering that change.

85
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:24,000
 So that sign-off is important and knowing where it does that.

86
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
 So three things you can do tomorrow.

87
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
 Find a constraint in your system.

88
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,000
 Probably isn't coding.

89
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 Probably isn't software engineers or cloud engineers or architects.

90
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,000
 Most of those have already got the tools that they need.

91
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:42,000
 You just need to work out how you make that fit your way of working.

92
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
 Put the agents where the people work.

93
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:50,000
 Make sure that they've got access to the agents in the tools that they use every day now.

94
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
 Don't make them move to a different tool.

95
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
 Or if you do, don't make them use a terminal.

96
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:00,000
 Trying to put a terminal in front of a product lead is really hard to sell.

97
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
 I tried.

98
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
 My partner's on.

99
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
 She wasn't happy.

100
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,000
 And then for every skill that you share, make sure that it's been verified.

101
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:15,000
 Make sure you've recorded how you verified it and what you tested it across.

102
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:20,000
 Running the Anthropic skill creator is great.

103
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,000
 The eval loop will cost you all of your tokens for that day.

104
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:28,000
 But actually it does produce some good output and it does do some good testing.

105
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:34,000
 I often say don't run eval loop and then regret it later.

